WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.269 --> 00:00:03.810
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:03.810 --> 00:00:08.930
In this series, we often talk about computer
animation and physical simulations, and these

00:00:08.930 --> 00:00:12.880
episodes are typically about one or the other.

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:18.810
You see, it is possible to teach a simulated
AI agent to lift weights and jump really high

00:00:18.810 --> 00:00:23.960
using physical simulations to make sure that
the movements and forces are accurate.

00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:26.960
The simulation side is always looking for
correctness.

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:31.780
However, let’s not forget that things also
have to look good.

00:00:31.780 --> 00:00:36.829
Animation studios are paying a fortune to
record motion capture data from real humans

00:00:36.829 --> 00:00:41.590
and sometimes even dogs to make sure that
these movements are visually appealing.

00:00:41.590 --> 00:00:47.140
So, is it possible to create something that
reacts to our commands with the controller,

00:00:47.140 --> 00:00:50.820
looks good, and also adheres to physics?

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:52.510
Well, have a look!

00:00:52.510 --> 00:00:55.160
This work was developed at Ubisoft La Forge.

00:00:55.160 --> 00:01:01.309
It responds to our input via the controller
and the output animations are fluid and natural.

00:01:01.309 --> 00:01:06.630
Since it relies on a technique called deep
reinforcement learning, it requires training.

00:01:06.630 --> 00:01:11.640
You see that early on, the blue agent is trying
to imitate the white character, and it is

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:13.280
not doing well at all.

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:17.869
It basically looks like me when going to bed
after reading papers all night.

00:01:17.869 --> 00:01:23.249
The white agent’s movement is not physically
simulated and was built using a motion database

00:01:23.249 --> 00:01:26.060
with only 10 minutes of animation data.

00:01:26.060 --> 00:01:29.240
This is the one that is in the “looks good”
category.

00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:34.439
Or, it would look really good if it wasn’t
pacing around like a drunkard, so the question

00:01:34.439 --> 00:01:39.579
naturally arises, who in their right minds
would control a character like this?

00:01:39.579 --> 00:01:41.920
Well, of course, no one!

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:47.079
This sequence was generated by an artificial,
worst-case player which is a nightmare situation

00:01:47.079 --> 00:01:48.810
for any AI to reproduce.

00:01:48.810 --> 00:01:51.990
Early on, it indeed is a nightmare.

00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:57.630
However…after 30 hours of training, the
blue agent learned to reproduce the motion

00:01:57.630 --> 00:02:01.459
of the white character, while being physically
simulated.

00:02:01.459 --> 00:02:04.169
So, what is the advantage of that?

00:02:04.169 --> 00:02:10.340
Well, for instance, it can interact with the
scene better, and is robust against perturbations.

00:02:10.340 --> 00:02:14.360
This means that it can rapidly recover from
undesirable positions.

00:02:14.360 --> 00:02:19.580
This can be validated via something that the
paper calls impact testing.

00:02:19.580 --> 00:02:21.240
Are you thinking what I am thinking?

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:27.380
I hope so, because I am thinking about throwing
blocks at this virtual agent, one of our favorite

00:02:27.380 --> 00:02:32.940
pastimes at Two Minute Papers, and it will
be able to handle them.

00:02:32.940 --> 00:02:33.940
Whoops!

00:02:33.940 --> 00:02:35.910
Well, most of them anyway.

00:02:35.910 --> 00:02:41.100
It also reacts to a change in direction much
quicker than previous agents.

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:46.640
If all that was not amazing enough, the whole
control system is very light, and takes only

00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:52.970
a few microseconds, most of which is spent
by not even the control part, but the physics

00:02:52.970 --> 00:02:53.970
simulation.

00:02:53.970 --> 00:02:59.070
So, with the power of computer graphics and
machine learning research, animation and physics

00:02:59.070 --> 00:03:04.510
can now be combined beautifully, it does not
limit controller responsiveness, looks very

00:03:04.510 --> 00:03:09.230
realistic, and it is very likely that we’ll
see this technique in action in future Ubisoft

00:03:09.230 --> 00:03:10.430
games.

00:03:10.430 --> 00:03:11.630
Outstanding.

